# lights-out — environment template
#
# Copy to .env and fill in one of the variables below:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is git-ignored and must never be committed. This template is the only
# tracked file that describes the expected keys, and it contains no secret.
#
# Resolution order for the GitHub token is fixed, highest precedence first:
#
#   1. LIGHTS_OUT_GITHUB_TOKEN   explicit override
#   2. GITHUB_TOKEN             injected automatically by GitHub Actions
#   3. .env file                this file, copied and filled in
#
# A real environment variable always beats the .env file, so a stray .env in a
# working directory can never shadow the token GitHub Actions provides.
#
# Only one of the two token variables needs to be set: leave LIGHTS_OUT_GITHUB_TOKEN
# empty to accept whatever GITHUB_TOKEN the environment already provides.

# --- Token -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Explicit override. Wins over GITHUB_TOKEN when both are set.
LIGHTS_OUT_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Standard name, also the key honoured inside .env. On GitHub Actions this is
# supplied by the workflow itself and does not need to be written down here.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# --- Optional overrides ----------------------------------------------------

# Path of the .env file consulted last. Defaults to ./.env.
# LIGHTS_OUT_ENV_FILE=.env

# Path of config.yaml. Defaults to ./config.yaml.
# LIGHTS_OUT_CONFIG=./config.yaml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required token permissions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The factory needs more than read access: it labels issues to claim them and to
# open pull requests. A token scoped to read-only cannot run the pipeline, and a
# missing write scope fails in the middle of a run rather than at start-up.
#
# Fine-grained personal access token — grant the minimum, and restrict
# "Repository access" to the single repository named in config.yaml:
#
#   Issues             Read and write   list issues, add/remove labels, comment
#   Pull requests      Read and write   open the pull request for a claim
#   Contents           Read-only        check out branches for worktrees
#
# Do not grant "Administration", "Workflows" or any organization-level scope:
# nothing in the factory uses them.
#
# Classic personal access token — the single "repo" scope covers all of the
# above for the selected repositories.
#
# The merge capability is deliberately absent from the code, so a token that
# could merge is a token the factory never exercises. Restricting it to the
# target repository is still worth doing.
